{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Only he shall not multiply horses for himself.</b> He says: even though you may have a king like the other kings of the nations, he shall diminish himself from the ways of other kings. For there are kings who strive in the multiplication of horses and chariots, but the king of Israel shall not multiply for himself horses, nor wives, nor silver and gold. There are kings who make these things the essence, but the king of Israel shall diminish in their multiplication and make the Torah and the fear of heaven the essence. This is what it says: ״והיתה עמו וקרא בו כל ימי חייו למען ילמד ליראה״ — 'and it shall be with him and he shall read in it all the days of his life, that he may learn to fear.' There are kings who glory in the abundance of their wealth and the greatness of their dominion, and their hearts are lifted up over all the world; but the king of Israel — ״לבלתי רום לבבו מאחיו״ — 'so that his heart not be lifted up above his brethren.' And Scripture assures him that in fulfilling this he shall lengthen days upon his kingdom. And our Rabbis of blessed memory explained that he shall not multiply horses for himself except for his chariot, that is, the chariot of his horsemen and soldiers, but he should not have two or three for his chariot, one time for this and one time for that. And the reason for the matter is written alongside it: ״ולא ישיב את העם מצרימה״ — 'and he shall not return the people to Egypt' — so that he not return the people to Egypt, for from there they would bring horses at that time. And thus we find in the matter of Solomon (1 Kings 10:29): ״ותצא מרכבה ממצרים בשש מאות כסף וסוס בחמשים ומאה״ — 'and a chariot came up and went out of Egypt for six hundred silver, and a horse for a hundred and fifty.'" "<b>And HaShem said to you: you shall not turn back to go on this way any more.</b> This is a commandment for the hour, so that Israel not learn from their deeds; for the Egyptians were known and renowned for every abomination, as it is written (Leviticus 18:3): ״כמעשה ארץ מצרים״ — 'like the deeds of the land of Egypt, etc.' Therefore He commanded them thus. And it is not a commandment for all generations, that Scripture would forbid dwelling in Egypt forever; for behold, we see many congregations dwelling there from then until this day. And had it been a commandment for all generations, the holy Israelites would not be lenient in this to dwell there, and had they done so, the sages of every generation would have protested against them." "<b>And there are those</b> who hold that it was a commandment for all generations, except that Scripture did not forbid dwelling in Egypt except to those who come there from the Land of Israel; and likewise the language of ״בדרך הזה״ — 'on this way' — indicates: 'that you are going,' that your intention was to the Land — you shall not turn back from it to Egypt.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:10.501404249Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}